WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.999
When you're building any application on Cloud, the system prompt is what shapes every response in the session.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
For this video, we'll cover what it does, how to structure it, and the practices that make it reliable.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.250
It lives before the human turn, it's invisible to end users, and getting it right is one of the

4
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.934
highest leveraged things you can do as a Cloud engineer.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Everything the model does in a session is shaped by a block of text the end user never sees.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.466
That block is the system prompt, passed to the model before any human message, and it establishes

7
00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:37.933
role, behavior, constraints, and background context.

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.322
The user turn is the human message in each exchange. It carries the immediate request and any data

9
00:00:44.322 --> 00:00:45.935
specific to that request.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.428
Persistent context is information that applies to every exchange in the session, placed once in the

11
00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:55.935
system prompt, rather than repeated in each user turn.

12
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.785
Getting that boundary right is what makes an application behave the same way on the 100th request as

13
00:01:01.785 --> 00:01:02.942
it did on the first.

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:10.000
One of the most important architectural decisions is knowing what goes in the system, prompt versus the user turn.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.329
The system prompt is for things that stay constant, the model's role, standing behavioral rules,

16
00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:21.934
formatting requirements, and background knowledge the model needs for every response.

17
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.533
A support bot's policy document is persistent, so it lives there. The user turn is for things that

18
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:32.933
change, the specific request, dynamic data, per request variables.

19
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.000
That same customer's account balance changes per user and per query, so it belongs in the user turn.

20
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:47.142
Mixing these two has real consequences. Dynamic data in the system prompt loses cache efficiency and

21
00:01:47.142 --> 00:01:50.928
gets resent and reprocessed on every single exchange,

22
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.236
driving up token cost. Standing rules in the user turn can be inadvertently overridden or ignored in

23
00:01:58.236 --> 00:02:01.927
a multi-turn conversation. Keep the boundary clean.

24
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:09.145
A well-structured system prompt has five sections, each doing a distinct job. Role defines who the

25
00:02:09.145 --> 00:02:15.927
model is, what expertise it brings, how it communicates, what professional identity it holds.

26
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.818
Context provides the background information the model needs for the whole session. Domain knowledge,

27
00:02:22.818 --> 00:02:24.931
company policy, reference data.

28
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.646
Instructions specify what the model should do. Task steps, response format, workflow behavior.

29
00:02:31.646 --> 00:02:38.929
Constraints define what the model should never do. Prohibited topics, tone restrictions, length limits.

30
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.862
Examples show how instructions translate to actual outputs. Each section answers a different

31
00:02:44.862 --> 00:02:51.936
question, and when a section is missing, the model fills that gap with defaults that may not match your intent.

32
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.232
There is a real tension between writing a thorough system prompt and controlling token cost. A

33
00:02:58.232 --> 00:03:03.933
thorough prompt covers edge cases, spells out formatting rules, and includes examples.

34
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.533
It reduces ambiguity and produces more consistent responses. But the full system prompt is re-sent

35
00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:16.933
with every request. So every additional word adds to your per-request, token count, and latency.

36
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:23.125
A minimal prompt is cheaper and faster. The risk is that the model fills unaddressed gaps with its

37
00:03:23.125 --> 00:03:26.937
own defaults, and those defaults may not match what you need.

38
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.808
The practical approach is to start with what you need for correctness, then trim language that is

39
00:03:32.808 --> 00:03:36.940
redundant or overly prescriptive, measuring consistency at each step.

40
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.175
The order of sections inside a system prompt matters more than most people expect, led with role

41
00:03:43.175 --> 00:03:47.935
because it frames how the model interprets every instruction that follows.

42
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.700
If the model knows it is a financial compliance analyst before it reads the instructions, those

43
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.940
instructions land differently than if role comes last.

44
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Instructions should come before constraints, so desired behavior is defined before limits are placed on it.

45
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.898
Examples should come last, illustrating rules already stated. Rather than front-loading patterns,

46
00:04:08.898 --> 00:04:11.939
the model has to reverse engineer into principles.

47
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.615
And when you are working with long documents, place that bulk reference material above your query

48
00:04:18.615 --> 00:04:23.942
and instructions, because putting the query at the end measurably improves response quality.

49
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.577
Conflicting instructions are one of the hardest bugs to catch in a system prompt because they don't

50
00:04:29.577 --> 00:04:31.943
always surface on the obvious test inputs.

51
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:39.000
A common example, telling the model to be concise while also instructing it to always explain its reasoning.

52
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.568
For simple tasks, these directives coexist fine. For complex tasks, they pull the model in opposite

53
00:04:45.568 --> 00:04:52.933
directions, and the output becomes inconsistent depending on which instruction the model weighted more heavily.

54
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
The fix is to use conditional phrasing. Be concise unless the user explicitly asks for an explanation.

55
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.000
That disambiguates the competing rules, rather than leaving the model to resolve them implicitly.

56
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Audit your prompt for pairs of rules that could simultaneously apply and rewrite them as conditionals.

57
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.000
System prompt testing should be treated as seriously as code testing.

58
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.000
The single happy path test case isn't enough.

59
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.085
You need to test across diverse phrasings of the same request to confirm that behavior is

60
00:05:26.085 --> 00:05:28.942
consistent, regardless of how users phrase things.

61
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:36.000
You need adversarial inputs that directly try to contradict your constraints to verify those constraints hold.

62
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:44.000
You need boundary conditions, very long inputs, empty inputs, sudden topic switches, in the middle of a session.

63
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.134
And every time you edit the system prompt, you need a regression pass to confirm that changes

64
00:05:49.134 --> 00:05:52.944
improved what you intended and didn't break what was already working.

65
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.000
A system prompt that hasn't been tested under pressure is an unknown risk.

66
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.000
A system prompt is the standing set of instructions sitting behind every turn in a session.

67
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.258
That's what makes it such a cheap place to change behavior, since one edit reaches every response

68
00:06:12.258 --> 00:06:13.935
your application produces.

69
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:20.236
Keep in mind, it also gets paid for on every request, and instructions that quietly contradict each

70
00:06:20.236 --> 00:06:21.937
other never throw an error.

71
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.000
They just make your output less reliable.

72
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
They show up as inconsistencies.

73
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.000
So that brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next one.

